  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1221 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nMonday is set to be the hottest day of the period as those\naforementioned mid-level height rises continue and peak, with \n500mb heights approaching 590 dam. Such values are above the 95th \npercentile of climatology for this time of year, and if this \nsignal persists it may warrant raising temperatures a few degrees \nabove the current NBM forecast (which has highs in the mid 80s to \nmid 90s). \n\nA slow-moving, closed upper low will be located over California on\nMonday, and as this system slowly shifts east it'll bring a return\nof precipitation chances to west Texas from Tues onward. An \ninitial look at precipitable water (PWAT) values from the Grand \nEnsemble indicates that values will be high (1.3-1.5\", which is \naround the 95th percentile of climatology). However, moderate \nmid- level southwesterly flow, with cloud layer mean wind speeds \naround 30 kts, will be strong enough to reduce the heavy rain \nconcerns outside of any training elements (which are difficult to \nresolve at this range). Across the entire Tues-Thurs period, the \nprobability of exceeding 1\" of rainfall is just 20-30% at any \ngiven location across the FA. Current timing suggests precip \nchances on Tuesday will be higher on the Caprock compared to off \nthe Caprock, and by Thursday the inverse will be true.\n\nAfter Monday, temps will cool over the course of next week,\naccentuated by a late week cold front that drops highs back into \nthe 60s and 70s on Friday. The post-frontal airmass should also \nfeature breezy northerly winds.",
